{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be your doctor during your stay. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been having chest pain, and it scared me. It started while I was doing the dishes—pressure in my chest that went to my back. I took nitroglycerin and it helped, but it’s come back a few times."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. You’ve had stents before, right? Any history of heart issues?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I had a stent placed in my right coronary artery a few years ago. I’ve had chest pain off and on since then, but this felt different. I also had nausea, vomiting, and dark stools two days ago."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And any weakness or dizziness?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes—my left side of my face has been drooping more, and I noticed my left leg felt weak and I was dizzy. But it’s a little better now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks. We did an EKG in the ER and saw some changes—T-wave inversions—which can be concerning for heart strain. We also noticed the facial droop, so we did a CT scan of your head to rule out a stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:27:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Was it a stroke? I’m really worried about that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT didn’t show any bleeding or stroke. But because of your symptoms, we’re doing an MRI and MRA to get a more detailed look at your brain and blood vessels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. What about my chest pain? Is my heart okay?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re being very careful. You’re on blood thinners for now, just in case. We’ll do a stress test tomorrow to check your heart’s blood flow. Because your blood pressure was high earlier, we’ll do a pharmacologic stress test instead of exercise."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had those before. They’re not fun, but I’ll do it if it helps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor you closely. Also, your labs show your kidneys are a bit stressed—your creatinine is up. That’s likely from dehydration due to vomiting and diarrhea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I didn’t realize that could affect my kidneys. Should I be worried?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s likely temporary—what we call prerenal failure. We’re giving you IV fluids slowly to help. We’ve paused your blood pressure meds that could affect the kidneys until things stabilize."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. What about my diabetes? I’ve been on metformin and glyburide."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve held your oral meds for now and are managing your sugars with insulin. Your A1C came back at 9.4, which means your blood sugar has been high lately. We’ll talk about adjusting that after you’re home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:44:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I know I haven’t been perfect with my diet. I’ll try harder."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re here to help. Also, your cholesterol levels are very high—LDL over 250. That increases your risk for heart events. We’re restarting a stronger statin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I think I stopped taking my statin because of muscle aches. Is the new one different?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2175-05-28 03:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re switching to atorvastatin 80 mg, which is more effective. If side effects come back, we can adjust. But controlling cholesterol is key for you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2175-05-28 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your MRI results are back. No sign of a stroke. There’s some chronic small vessel disease in the brain, which is common with high blood pressure and diabetes, but nothing new."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2175-05-28 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good news. What about the narrowing in my neck arteries?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2175-05-28 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We did a carotid ultrasound. There’s plaque, but no severe blockage—less than 40% on both sides. So no procedure needed. The facial droop is likely a reactivation of your old Bell’s palsy, possibly triggered by the recent illness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2175-05-28 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had Bell’s palsy before. I thought it was gone. Can it come back like this?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2175-05-28 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can recur, especially with stress or illness. Since your neurologic workup is reassuring, we don’t think it’s a stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We just got your stress test results. It’s normal—no signs of reduced blood flow to your heart. The ejection fraction is 53%, and no ischemia was seen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart causing the chest pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not this time. The pain could be related to your known coronary disease, but it’s not showing active blockage. We’ll keep you on your heart meds—aspirin, Plavix, beta-blocker, and nitrate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad it’s not a blockage. What about the GI symptoms? I haven’t vomited since I got here."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your exam was benign, no signs of infection or bleeding. We think it was a viral illness. Since you’re not having symptoms now, we’ll monitor and restart your stomach meds like Prilosec and sucralfate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:40:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had ulcers before, so I’m careful with NSAIDs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2175-05-28 14:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. Avoid them. Your chest X-ray was clear too—no pneumonia or fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Your kidney function is improving. Creatinine is coming down. We’ll restart your lisinopril and a lower dose of HCTZ today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I can go home today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, if all goes well. You’re stable, no chest pain, and your tests are reassuring. We’ll set you up with home services for a few days to help with recovery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What do I need to do when I get home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take all your meds as listed—especially the new statin and insulin. Follow up with your primary doctor in a few days. Call us if you have chest pain, weakness, or dizziness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my face? Will it get better?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It should improve as the inflammation settles. Give it time. Keep managing your risk factors—diabetes, blood pressure, cholesterol."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better knowing it wasn’t a heart attack or stroke."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2175-05-29 07:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’re discharging you with a plan. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2175-05-28 02:37:00",
    "admission_end": "2175-05-29 17:55:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 60-year-old woman with a history of coronary artery disease, diabetes, and hypertension was admitted for chest pain, left facial droop, and gastrointestinal symptoms. She had recent nausea, vomiting, and dark stools, followed by substernal chest pressure relieved by nitroglycerin. In the ED, ECG showed T-wave inversions, and she was noted to have left facial droop and prior left leg weakness. CT head was negative for stroke, and MRI/MRA showed no acute ischemia but chronic small vessel disease and mild left carotid siphon narrowing. Carotid ultrasound revealed less than 40% stenosis bilaterally. A Persantine MIBI stress test showed normal perfusion and ejection fraction without ischemia. Her chest pain was deemed non-acute, likely related to known coronary disease. The facial droop was attributed to recurrent Bell’s palsy. Acute kidney injury, likely prerenal from dehydration, improved with IV fluids. Her diabetes was managed with insulin due to held oral agents, and her very high cholesterol led to initiation of high-dose atorvastatin. She was discharged home with home services on optimized medical therapy.",
    "problems": [
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Acute kidney failure, unspecified",
      "Intermediate coronary syndrome",
      "Other and unspecified hyperlipidemia",
      "Bell's palsy",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status"
    ]
  }
}
